      "turn": 1,
      "user": "When a term deposit matures, should I move some into stocks?",
      "status": 200,
      "content": "Here's the plain answer: a term deposit is a fixed-term savings product, and when it matures you get your money back plus interest. Whether to move some of it into stocks is your choice, and it depends on what the money is for.\n\nYour term deposits ($300,000) are counted as Safety, the low-risk part that steadies the whole picture when stocks or crypto fall. That's why Safety is kept apart from stocks: its job is to be steadier, not to grow fastest. If you move money out of Safety into stocks, the balance tilts toward the riskier part, and stock prices can fall right when you need the cash.\n\nYour emergency fund is separate from all of this. It's already full, so it doesn't depend on when your deposit matures.",
